//@version=6

// ============================================================================
// V9.1 — ADDENDUM TO THE V9 PATCH
// ============================================================================
//
// JCs_Indicators_Lib_V9_patch.pine IS NOT MODIFIED BY THIS FILE. V9 stands as
// written; this is a second, later layer applied on top of it. Where this file
// and V9 disagree, V9.1 wins, for the same reason V9 wins over the library.
//
// Two kinds of change, kept apart because they are not the same claim:
//
//   SECTION C — TWO BUGS V9 DID NOT CATCH, in the same class as the ones it
//     did. Each is a function whose signals cannot mean what the slot needs
//     them to mean. Found by running the ported library over 28 pairs and
//     23 years and looking at what each indicator actually says.
//
//   SECTION D — FOUR DEFAULTS RE-DECLARED. Not bugs in the code: values that
//     were chosen while the parameter they set was dead or unreachable, and
//     which leave the indicator saying nothing at all. A default picked while
//     the code was inert is not a real default, and an indicator that takes no
//     trades cannot enter gate 1 — it would be scored "no edge" when it was
//     never asked a question.
//
// THE PYTHON PORT USES SECTION C AND SECTION D FROM DAY ONE. The as-written
// versions are retained ONLY for TradingView parity comparisons, behind an
// explicit flag, because a parity run has to reproduce what TradingView does
// rather than what it should do. Nothing else may call them.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// C1  supertrend_signals              read ta.supertrend's direction backwards
// C2  chandelier_exit_signals         confirmed both directions at once
//
// D1  rex_oscillator                  oscillator and signal line identical
// D2  volatility_quality              dead band ~859x the values it gates
// D3  damiani_volatmeter              computes an input then discards it
// D4  glitch_index                    threshold set while the parameter was dead
// ============================================================================


// ############################################################################
// SECTION C — TWO MORE BUGS
// ############################################################################


// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// C1 — Supertrend direction is inverted
//
// WAS: lc = dir > 0, sc = dir < 0.
//
// ta.supertrend returns the direction as -1 WHEN THE TREND IS UP and +1 when
// it is down. That is TradingView's documented convention, not a quirk of this
// chart. So the helper confirmed LONG while the supertrend line sat above
// price, and short while it sat below: every trade taken on Supertrend as C1,
// C2 or exit was on the wrong side of it.
//
// Measured on the ported library, 28 pairs, 1999-2026: as written it confirms
// long on 52.3% of bars and short on 47.6%, which looks perfectly healthy and
// is exactly backwards. Nothing in the shape of the output reveals this — only
// reading TradingView's sign convention does.
//
// NOW: the sign is read the way ta.supertrend defines it.
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
export supertrend_signals(simple float factor, simple int atr_period) =>
	[stv, dir] = ta.supertrend(factor, atr_period)
	// -1 is UP. This is the whole fix.
	lt = dir < 0 and dir[1] > 0
	st = dir > 0 and dir[1] < 0
	lc = dir < 0
	sc = dir > 0
	[lt, st, lc, sc]

export supertrend_exit(simple float factor, simple int atr_period) =>
	[stv, dir] = ta.supertrend(factor, atr_period)
	le = dir > 0 and dir[1] < 0
	se = dir < 0 and dir[1] > 0
	[le, se]


// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// C2 — Chandelier Exit confirms both directions at once
//
// WAS: lc = close > ClongStop, sc = close < CshortStop.
//
// ClongStop is the highest high minus a multiple of ATR and therefore sits
// BELOW price; CshortStop is the lowest low plus the same and sits ABOVE it.
// Whenever price is between its own two trailing stops — the ordinary case —
// BOTH conditions are true. Measured: 51.7% of bars confirm long and short
// simultaneously.
//
// As C2 that is not a filter at all. It votes for whatever C1 says and can
// never disagree with it, which is precisely the fault V9's A5 fixed in
// schaff_trend_cycle_signals ("lc and sc were both true mid-range"). A5 caught
// one instance; this is the other.
//
// NOW: resolved to a single direction the same way A5 did it — the indicator
// already maintains `Cdir`, a proper one-or-the-other state that flips when
// price crosses a stop, and the confirmation reads THAT. The stop levels stay
// exactly as they were; only what the helper reports changes.
//
// A note on why this is not merely narrowing: with Cdir the indicator becomes
// binary (always long or short after warm-up) rather than a thing that agrees
// with everything. The triggers were already Cdir-based and are unchanged.
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
export chandelier_exit_signals(simple int atr_period, float atr_mult, bool use_close) =>
	a = chandelier_exit(atr_period, atr_mult, use_close)
	ce_bs = array.get(a, 2)
	lt = ce_bs == 1.0  and nz(ce_bs[1], 1.0) != 1.0
	st = ce_bs == -1.0 and nz(ce_bs[1], 1.0) != -1.0
	lc = ce_bs == 1.0
	sc = ce_bs == -1.0
	[lt, st, lc, sc]

export chandelier_exit_exit(simple int atr_period, float atr_mult, bool use_close) =>
	a = chandelier_exit(atr_period, atr_mult, use_close)
	ce_bs = array.get(a, 2)
	le = ce_bs == -1.0 and nz(ce_bs[1], 1.0) != -1.0
	se = ce_bs == 1.0  and nz(ce_bs[1], 1.0) != 1.0
	[le, se]


// ############################################################################
// SECTION D — FOUR DEFAULTS RE-DECLARED
// ############################################################################
//
// Each of these ran, produced no signal, and would have entered a 17.6M-
// combination sweep contributing nothing while looking like a tested option.
// Every replacement below is either the indicator's published setting or, where
// the construction has no published form, a stated choice with its reason.
// All four keep their inert_at_defaults tag in the registry so the record shows
// the value was re-declared rather than inherited.


// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// D1 — Rex Oscillator: rex_smooth 13 / signal 13  ->  14 / 22
//
// The strategy sets rex_c1_t1 = "SMA", rex_c1_l1 = 13, rex_c1_t2 = "SMA",
// rex_c1_l2 = 13. Both the oscillator and its signal line are the same moving
// average, of the same length, over the same series. They are one array
// compared against itself: rex_ma > signal_ma is never true, and neither is
// the reverse. Measured over 28 pairs and 23 years: 0% long, 0% short, 100%
// neutral, ZERO triggers. Any combination selecting Rex takes no trades.
//
// PUBLISHED FORM. Rex smooths the True Value Bar (close - low + close - open
// - (high - close)) over 14 periods and takes a 9-period signal line OF THAT
// AVERAGE. This implementation cannot do that: its signal line reads the raw
// TVB, not the oscillator. Two cascaded simple averages of 14 and 9 have the
// single-average equivalent length 14 + 9 - 1 = 22, so 14 and 22 preserve both
// published constants under this structure.
//
// The ORDER matters and is the reason for not simply writing 14 and 9. The
// oscillator must be the FASTER leg, or lc = rex_ma > signal_ma reads a slow
// average above a fast one, which is falling momentum reported as bullish —
// the C1 inversion of Section C all over again.
//
// Measured at 14 / 22: 49.8% long, 49.9% short, ~1,020 triggers per pair.
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// rex_smooth_length          : 13 -> 14
// rex_signal_smoothing       : 13 -> 22
// (rex_ma_type and rex_signal_ma_type stay "SMA")


// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// D2 — Volatility Quality: vq_atr_percentage 7.5 -> 0.075
//
// inpFilter is a DEAD BAND, not a threshold: a new reading is accepted only if
// it moves more than inpFilter x ATR away from the last accepted one. But
// `val` is a ratio multiplied by a price difference — median magnitude
// 0.0000839 on EURUSD — while 7.5 x ATR is 0.0720. A new value is accepted
// only on a jump of about 859x the typical one. The indicator takes THREE
// distinct values across 23 years, fires zero triggers, and confirms long on
// 99.8% of bars purely because of whichever value it froze at.
//
// The strategy labels this input "Volatility Quality: ATR %". Read as the
// percentage it says it is, 7.5 means 0.075 — and the units then make sense:
// a dead band of 7.5% of ATR. That is the smallest possible reading of the
// author's intent, changing no number, only where the decimal point sits.
//
// Measured at 0.075: 45.6% long, 54.2% short, ~92 triggers per pair. At 0.0
// (dead band off) it is an ordinary oscillator with ~990; the band is kept
// because the author put it there deliberately.
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// vq_atr_percentage          : 7.5 -> 0.075


// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// D3 — Damiani Volatmeter: THIS IS A BUG, not just a default
//
// The library computes std_short and std_long, forms std_ratio from them, and
// then never reads it:
//
//     vol_ratio = atr(viscosity) / atr(sedimentation)
//     std_ratio = stdev(close, viscosity) / stdev(close, sedimentation)   // unused
//     vmeter    = use_lag ? vol_ratio - lag : vol_ratio
//     tradable  = vmeter > threshold
//
// With the shipped 7 / 50 / 1.4 / 1.4 that asks for atr(7)/atr(50) > 2.8. Over
// 28 pairs and 23 years that ratio reaches 5.775 once and sits near 1.0
// otherwise: the filter passes 0.044% of bars and blocks essentially every
// trade.
//
// Damiani's published construction uses the std ratio as an ANTI-THRESHOLD —
// it lowers the bar the volatility ratio has to clear, rather than being
// discarded and replaced by a constant subtraction:
//
//     tradable = vol_ratio > threshold - std_ratio
//
// which is what the unused variable was computed for. Measured that way, with
// the SAME published 7 / 50 / 1.4: passes 42.9% of bars, range 40.3% to 45.9%
// across the 28 pairs. A filter, as intended.
//
// The viscosity, sedimentation and threshold defaults are unchanged; `lag` and
// `use_lag` become inert under the corrected form and are retained only so the
// signature does not change.
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
export damiani_volatmeter(simple int viscosity, simple int sedimentation, float threshold, float lag, bool use_lag) =>
	vol_atr_short = ta.atr(viscosity)
	vol_atr_long  = ta.atr(sedimentation)
	std_short     = ta.stdev(close, viscosity)
	std_long      = ta.stdev(close, sedimentation)
	vol_ratio = vol_atr_long != 0 ? vol_atr_short / vol_atr_long : 0.0
	// the anti-threshold. This is the line the original computed and dropped.
	anti_thres = std_long != 0 ? std_short / std_long : 0.0
	vmeter   = vol_ratio
	eff_thres = threshold - anti_thres
	tradable = vmeter > eff_thres
	[vmeter, eff_thres, tradable]


// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// D4 — Glitch Index: multiplier 5.0 -> 1.0
//
// V9's A7 rewrote this indicator. It had been a z-score times a constant that
// was then sign-tested, so the multiplier could not change a single trade — a
// dead parameter. A7 made it a real threshold on a displacement measured in
// units of average range, and correctly made the indicator ternary.
//
// But 5.0 was chosen when the number could not matter. Under the new
// construction the displacement's 99th percentile is 5.92, so a threshold of
// 5.0 is crossed on 2.4% of bars and the indicator is neutral 97.6% of the
// time. As C1 it blocks almost every entry — inert for a different reason than
// D1-D3, but inert.
//
// NO PUBLISHED DEFAULT EXISTS for A7's construction; it is a new formula, so
// there is nothing to look up and this is a proposal. The value is chosen so
// the indicator does the job A7 states — "decline to confirm either side" when
// price is near its own average — with a neutral share in line with the other
// ternary confirmations already in the library:
//
//     ADX/DMI 63.9%   TDFI 60.7%   Ichimoku 32.4%   LinReg slope 29.5%
//     Bears/Bulls Impulse 30.9%    Glitch @ 1.0 -> 39.4%
//
// At 1.0 it is 31.8% long, 28.7% short, 39.4% neutral. At the old 5.0 it is
// 1.0 / 0.8 / 98.2. Anything below ~0.5 stops it being selective at all.
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// glitch_index multiplier    : 5.0 -> 1.0
